{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-158743","model_call":95,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q79"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q79"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_556245902#0_1166749769","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q79","rank":7,"snippet":"updated May 18 2018 REVOLUTIONS OF 1848 origins and background the outbreak and spread of revolution possibilities and problems confrontations and decisions struggles and accomplishments bibliography Of the major waves of <cover>revolution in modern European history—1789 to 1799, 1848 to 1851, 1917 to 1923, and 1989 to 1991—those of the mid-nineteenth century extended across the largest territory and among the greatest diversity of political and socioeconomic regimes. The 1848 revolutions occurred in lands from the Atlantic to Ukraine, and from the Baltic to the Black Sea, including France, Prussia, Austria, Bavaria, as well as the smaller and midsized German states; the Kingdoms of the Two Sicilies and the Kingdom of Piedmont-Sardinia, the Papal States, and the smaller Italian states; and, in the far southeastern corner of Europe, the Danubian Principalities of Moldavia and Walachia. The revolutionary movement had a strong effect on the Scandinavian lands and the Low Countries, with major mass movements in Norway, and important reforms in Denmark, Belgium, and the Netherlands. </cover>The states of the Iberian Peninsula and the two peripheral Great Powers, the Russian and British Empires, were the only parts of Europe not directly affected in their domestic politics. The 1848 ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_556245902#0_1166749769","rank":7,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":95,"document_judgments":38,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
